<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Indus River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Indus is a great river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> flowing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>. Its valley supported one of the earliest civilisations. It marked the eastern limit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> and of Alexander's Macedonian conquests, and was reached by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a> in their campaigns.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indus.jpg" alt="Indus" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heavyrunner · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nanga_Parbat_Indus_Gorge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
